This peer isolates the Boolean algebra of subsets generated by the binary-coordinate sets of the natural numbers and records the finite bit-mask calculations used later.

<!-- formalization -->

**Definition (`GeneratedBy`).** A set is generated by a sequence of generators when it can be obtained from individual generators and the whole space by finitely many intersections, unions, and complements.

**Definition (`bitmaskOf`).** The bit mask of a finite set of natural numbers is the sum of the corresponding powers of two.

**Theorem (`testBit_sum_two_pow`).** The \(i\)-th bit of the sum of distinct powers \(2^j\), with \(j\) ranging over a finite set, is set exactly when \(i\) belongs to that set.

*Proof.* Distinct binary place values give precisely the listed bit indices. ∎

**Theorem (`testBit_bitmaskOf`).** The \(i\)-th bit of the bit mask of a finite set is set exactly when \(i\) belongs to the set.

*Proof.* This is the preceding characterization applied to the defining sum. ∎

**Definition (`generator`).** The \(i\)-th generator is the set of natural numbers whose \(i\)-th binary digit is one.

**Theorem (`mem_generator`).** A natural number belongs to the \(i\)-th generator exactly when its \(i\)-th binary digit is one.

*Proof.* This is immediate from the definition of the generator. ∎
